NAME
    m - initialize the technical runtime and dispatch native commands

SYNOPSIS
    m
    m <command> [argument ...]

DESCRIPTION
    m resolves the technical runtime root, initializes the relocatable m
    runtime environment, establishes the current state/resource roots, directly
    loads core.lib.sh, sources any materialized global execution environment,
    exposes the final technical command path, and then performs command dispatch
    or managed-shell entry.

    With no command operand, m starts the managed shell through the shell
    facility.

    With a command operand, m attempts to resolve and canonicalize the first
    operand to reject recursive execution of the bootstrap. It then executes
    the original argument vector externally via its native interpreter, with
    exported initialized m environment and PATH. m does not interpret command
    shebangs, source command bodies, or export a per-command identity.

    Environment-dependent commands use their native shebangs, require nonempty
    m_ROOT, and load any shell libraries they need inside their own process.
    To invoke one from an uninitialized shell, use m <command>. A command
    already inheriting initialized m environment can be invoked directly.

    The bootstrap refuses to dispatch itself recursively as a command.

GLOBAL EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT
    Before command dispatch, m reads generated environment state from:

        state/system/current/sys/environment/cache

    relative to m_ROOT.

    When present, m sources the regular file:

        env

    and then the platform selector:

        env-osarch -> env-<osarch>

    m tests each expected pathname with -f and sources it when that test
    succeeds. It does not validate or regenerate env-osarch selector structure
    during bootstrap.

    These files are derived state. m does not resolve package providers,
    facility defaults or package defaults while bootstrapping.

    Provider PATH contributions are applied to the inherited host PATH by the
    generated files. m then prepends its technical sys/ext command roots, so the
    final technical precedence is:

        m technical command roots
        selected-osarch provider PATH
        osarch-independent provider PATH
        inherited host PATH

CORE-PROVIDED RUNTIME FUNCTIONS
    Once a shell process loads core.lib.sh, that shell provides the following
    functions. They are not inherited by independently executed commands:
    each shell command needing them loads core.lib.sh itself.

    loadlib <library-reference>
        Load exactly one m-owned POSIX-shell library reference relative to
        m_LIB_DIR. The reference omits the final .lib.sh suffix.

        The resolved file must be a readable regular file and is sourced in the
        current shell environment.

        Return status:
            loaded library status
                returned unchanged after a successful load
            1   invalid invocation; exactly one reference is required
            2   resolved library file is missing, not regular, or unreadable

    loadsyslib <system-shell-library-reference>
        Load exactly one m system shell library below lib/sys/sh by delegating
        to loadlib with the sys/sh/ prefix.

        Examples:

            loadsyslib "array"
            loadsyslib "pkg/pkg-install"
            loadsyslib "rsudo/rsudo-mod-fs"

        Positional-parameter forwarding is not part of the loading interface.
        m- and RumiAI-owned shell code uses loadsyslib for owned lib/sys/sh
        dependencies; runtime/external pathname sourcing remains ordinary
        POSIX dot-sourcing.

        Return status:
            delegated loadlib/library status

EXIT STATUS
    With a dispatched command, m returns that command's status.
    Bootstrap, root-resolution or command-resolution failures return non-zero.

